col24                             3\ncol25                             3\ncol27                             9\ncol28                             5\ncol29                             3\ncol30                             2\ndtype: int64\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":9},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"\n#### 1. Primary Identifiers & Target (The Core)\n* **`TransactionID`** *(138,701 unique)*: The unique receipt or auction ID for that specific sale. Because it has exactly 138,701 unique values, this matches the total row count. It is simply an index and should be **dropped** before training the model.\n* **`TargetValue`** *(675 unique)*: This is what the models are trying to predict. In this domain, it is almost certainly the Sale Price or Auction Value of the machine.\n* **`AssetID`** *(120,111 unique)*: The unique ID of the specific machine. Notice this number is smaller than `TransactionID`? That means some machines were sold, used, and then resold at auction more than once!\n\n#### 2. Machine Specs & Physical Properties\n*These features define exactly what kind of machine is being sold.*\n* **`ProductConfigID`**: A code representing the specific configuration/model setup of the machine.\n* **`ManufactureYear`**: The year the machine was built. *(Note: Watch out for the `1000`/`1001` outlier we found earlier!)*\n* **`DrivetrainType`** *(8 unique)*: How power moves to the wheels/tracks (e.g., Four-Wheel Drive, Two-Wheel Drive, Track-type).\n* **`CabinType`** *(3 unique)*: The operator's environment (e.g., Open Canopy, Enclosed Cab with A/C). This heavily impacts price!\n* **`Forks`** *(2 unique)*: Indicates if the machine has a forklift attachment setup (True/False or Yes/No).\n* **`Spec_BaseClass`** & **`Spec_SubClass`**: The category of machine (e.g., \"Excavator\", \"Track Dozer\", \"Skid Steer\").\n\n#### 3. Usage & Condition (The \"Odometer\")\n*Just like a car's mileage, these tell  how hard the machine was worked.*\n* **`OperationalHoursMeter`** *(14,643 unique)*: The machine's odometer. It measures how many total hours the engine has run. High hours usually translate to a lower price.\n* **`UtilizationTier`** *(3 unique)*: A simplified rating of how heavily the asset was used (e.g., \"Low\", \"Medium\", \"High\"). An `OrdinalEncoder` is perfect for this column.\n\n#### 4. Sales & Logistics Data\n* **`TransactionDate`** *(3,676 unique)*: The exact day the machine was sold. <br>\n  *Crucial Step: Do not feed this raw text date into your model. Extract features from it like `SaleYear`, `SaleMonth`, and `Age_At_Sale` (which is `SaleYear - ManufactureYear`).*\n* **`RegionCode`** *(53 unique)*: The geographic area or state where the auction took place (likely US states + a few Canadian provinces). Prices vary depending on local industrial demand!\n* **`VendorPartnerID`** *(31 unique)*: The dealer or auction house broker managing the sale.\n\n#### 5. The Anonymized Columns (`col1` to `col30`)\nColumns like `col20`, `col21`, etc., have been intentionally renamed or masked by the provider to protect proprietary business secrets. However, based on their values, we can decode what some of them represent:\n* **Tracks & Shoes**: Columns containing \"Steel\" or \"Rubber\" are likely tracking types (e.g., Track Width or Shoe Type).\n* **Attachment Sizes**: Columns containing \"9' 6\\\"\" or \"20 inch\" are attachment sizes (e.g., Bucket width, Blade size).\n* **Configuration Types**: Columns containing \"Triple\" or \"Double\" refer to the Mast Type on a forklift or the grouser type on an excavator track.\n\n> ** Handling NaNs in Anonymized Columns:**\n> \n> Notice the high frequency of `NaN` values in `col20` through `col30`. These features only apply to specific types of machines (e.g., a bulldozer doesn't have a forklift mast).  `SimpleImputer` will likely need to treat these missing values as a distinct category, such as `\"None\"` or `\"Missing\"`.\n","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"with pd.option_co